## Clip a raster

Our area of interest (AOI) for this lesson is a smaller region that includes only a few blocks around the NCEAS building. 
This is outlined in a GeoJSON file:

::: {#32a8eb8b .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
fp = os.path.join('data', 'SB_aoi.geojson')
aoi = gpd.read_file(fp)
aoi.plot()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](lesson-15-rioxarray_files/figure-html/cell-12-output-1.png){width=508 height=411}
:::
:::


Remember: **if two geospatial sets will interact they need to be in the same CRS**. In our case, the AOI `geopandas.GeoDataFrame` does not have the same CRS as the rasters:

::: {#8e37ee48 .cell execution_count=12}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Examine CRSs
print('aoi CRS: ', aoi.crs)
print('nir CRS: ', nir.rio.crs)
print('rgb CRS: ', rgb.rio.crs)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
aoi CRS:  EPSG:4326
nir CRS:  EPSG:26911
rgb CRS:  EPSG:26911
```
:::
:::


So let's reproject:

::: {#9b25c897 .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Reproject AOI to RGB CRS
aoi = aoi.to_crs(rgb.rio.crs)
print('Matched CRS?',  aoi.crs == rgb.rio.crs)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Matched CRS? True
```
:::
:::


And plot them together:

::: {#be840330 .cell execution_count=14}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Plot of RGB raster with AOI overlay
fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 6 * rgb_aspect_ratio))  # Directly set size and aspect
rgb.plot.imshow(ax=ax)
aoi.plot(ax=ax, alpha=0.6)
plt.show()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](lesson-15-rioxarray_files/figure-html/cell-15-output-1.png){width=354 height=414}
:::
:::


To clip the raster to the AOI's bounding box we use the `.rio.clip_box()` method:

::: {#650d31bc .cell execution_count=15}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Clip rasters to AOI
rgb_small = rgb.rio.clip_box(*aoi.total_bounds)
nir_small = nir.rio.clip_box(*aoi.total_bounds)
```
:::


Notice a few things:

- we had to use the `.rio` accessor to access the `clip_box()` method 
- [`.rio.clip_box()` usual parameters](https://corteva.github.io/rioxarray/stable/rioxarray.html#rioxarray.raster_dataset.RasterDataset.clip_box) are `minx`, `miny`, `maxx`, `maxy`, the same four values the `total_bounds` attribute gave us when we [clipped with a bounding box using `gpd.clip()`](/book/chapters/lesson-13-clipping/lesson-13-clipping.qmd#clipping-with-a-bounding-box). We are using the `*` asterisk as an unpacking operator to get these from the array `aoi.total_bounds`.

Let's check our clipped data:

::: {#a4fc8cad .cell execution_count=16}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Examine clipped data 
print('Original shape: ', nir.shape)
print('Clipped shape: ', nir_small.shape)

nir_small.plot()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Original shape:  (3208, 2419)
Clipped shape:  (1710, 1995)
```
:::

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display}
![](lesson-15-rioxarray_files/figure-html/cell-17-output-2.png){width=605 height=464}
:::
:::


